<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josiah Harlan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Josiah Harlan was an American adventurer who travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in the early nineteenth century. He was among the foreign officers employed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>. He is mentioned alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-allard">Allard</a>, Court and Ventura as one of the Europeans and Americans who helped train the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josiah-harlan.jpg" alt="Josiah Harlan" width="216" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JosiahHarlan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Josiah Harlan was born on 12 June 1799 in Newlin Township, Chester County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, one of ten children raised in a strict Quaker household; his brother Richard Harlan became a paleontologist. He read widely, spoke French and read Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and took a particular interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. In 1820 he sailed as supercargo on a merchant ship between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. When his fiancée, Elizabeth Swaim, married another man, he vowed never to return to America.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In July 1824, with no medical training, he was hired as a surgeon by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and served in the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from January 1825, sometimes fighting with the Bengal Artillery. Reading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mountstuart-elphinstone">Mountstuart Elphinstone</a>'s account of the Kingdom of Caubul drew him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. In 1829 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, where the French general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-allard">Jean-François Allard</a> introduced him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a>.</p><p>After a falling-out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, in 1836. A letter in English sent to Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> in Dost Mohammad's name that year, asking for help to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, has a style and Americanisms that strongly suggest Harlan wrote it. In 1838 he led an expedition of some 1,400 cavalry and 1,100 infantry across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> slave trader Muhammad Murad Beg, and claimed he was given the title Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a> in return for military aid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> he published A Memoir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 1842, attacking his enemies in India and arguing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> could easily harm the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>; it was denounced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In the Civil War he raised the 11th Pennsylvania Cavalry and became its colonel, but after a court-martial and illness he was invalided out on 19 August 1862. He worked as a doctor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> and died of tuberculosis in October 1871.</p><p>Many critics see him as a model for Daniel Dravot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudyard-kipling">Rudyard Kipling</a>'s story The Man Who Would Be King of 1888: both were Freemasons who entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> disguised as holy men and were granted Afghan titles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Josiah Harlan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Newlin Township, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.93,-75.74&amp;year=1845" aria-label="Newlin Township on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2684 735 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Josiah Harlan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3134 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3134 1005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.93,-75.74&amp;year=1845">What was here in 1845? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
